        <p class="src-note reveal">
          Share-price gains over the 2026 TSX Venture 50 ranking period. What these names did says
          nothing about what they do next — the point is how big the moves get here, not a suggestion
          to buy any of them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ WHY TORONTO ============ -->
    <section class="sec">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">Why the paperwork ends up here</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          A company with a copper deposit in Peru or a lithium project in Nevada could list almost
          anywhere. Plenty pick Canada. It's not patriotism. The listing rules were written for
          companies that have a rock and a plan rather than a profit. The disclosure standard has
          teeth. NI 43-101 exists because of Bre-X, and everyone here still remembers why. And the
          brokers on Bay Street and Howe Street have been financing mines for a hundred years.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          The result is a home market that looks like nowhere else. Gold, silver, copper, uranium,
          lithium, the rare earths — the whole critical-minerals list trades right here, in companies
          small enough that one drill hole moves them 60% in an afternoon.
        </p>

        <figure class="reveal">
          <img
            src={mapFig.src}
            width={FIG_W}
            height={mapH}
            alt="A printed atlas plate marking Toronto and Vancouver, with three reasons global mining companies list in Canada: listing rules built for exploration-stage companies, a century of mine finance, and a disclosure standard others copy."
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Three reasons a Peruvian copper project ends up filing in Ontario instead of Lima.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ WHERE THE MONEY COMES FROM ============ -->
    <section class="sec">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">Where the money actually comes from</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          A junior has no revenue and no dividend, so there are only three ways it ever pays you.
          <strong>One:</strong> a hole hits and the market re-rates the whole company overnight.
          <strong>Two:</strong> a major runs short of its own ounces and turns up with a cheque.
          <strong>Three:</strong> the metal itself runs, and everything holding that metal in the
          ground gets dragged along. All three are live at once right now, which is not the normal
          state of this market.
        </p>

        <div class="rerate reveal">
          <div class="pair">
            <span class="n">C$1.17</span>
            <span class="arw">→</span>
            <span class="n hi">C$29.00</span>
          </div>
          <p class="cap">
            Great Bear announced the Hinge Zone in August 2018 and closed that day at C$1.17 — up
            62.5% on one news release. Three years and a lot of drilling later, Kinross paid C$29.00
            a share for the company: C$1.8 billion, a 40% premium to the previous 20-day average,
            plus a C$1.00 contingent value right, one more dollar a share if Dixie ever goes into
            production.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="body reveal">
          The second route is worth understanding, because it isn't luck — it's structural. The
          majors gutted their own exploration teams decades ago. They still have to replace every
          ounce they mine, and they can't find it themselves anymore, so they buy it. They pay up to
          get there before a rival does, and that premium is your exit.
        </p>

        <div class="tbl-scroll reveal">
          <table class="deal-tbl">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Target</th>
                <th>What the buyer was really after</th>
                <th>The cheque</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {deals.map((d, i) => (
                <tr style={`transition-delay:${i * 90}ms`}>
                  <td>{d.who}<br /><span class="tk">{d.tk}</span></td>
                  <td data-l="What the buyer got">{d.got}</td>
                  <td class="cheque" data-l="The cheque"><strong>{d.lead}</strong> {d.rest}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="src-note reveal">
          Three out of dozens. The pattern repeats every cycle, and it speeds up when metal prices
          sit where they sit today.
        </p>

        <div class="acts reveal" style="margin-top:28px">
          <button type="button" class="btn link-btn" onclick="onLinkBtnClick()">
            Get the morning notes free
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h13M13 6l6 6-6 6" /></svg>
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ THE CHAIN ============ -->
    <section class="sec">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">The chain: a number in Washington, a stock in the Yukon</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Every few weeks a number comes out of Washington that has nothing to do with mining and
          moves every mining stock in this space anyway — a jobs report, an inflation print,
          eventually a line from the Fed itself. That's not a detour from the story. It's upstream
          of it.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Gold pays no dividend and no interest. The only cost of holding it is the yield you give
          up by holding it instead of a bond — so anything that changes what the market expects the
          Fed to do next changes gold's price that same day, before the Fed has said a word. Core
          inflation cooled from a seven-month high of 2.9% in May to 2.6% in June, better than
          economists expected, and the odds on a cut moved with it. Even so, the Fed hasn't cut
          once in 2026. The policy rate has sat at 3.50%–3.75% since December, and Goldman Sachs
          has already trimmed its own year-end gold call by US$500 an ounce on the bet that it
          stays there.
        </p>

        <div class="rerate reveal">
          <div class="pair">
            <span class="n">2.9%</span>
            <span class="arw">→</span>
            <span class="n hi">2.6%</span>
          </div>
          <p class="cap">
            U.S. core inflation, May to June 2026. Gold's held above US$4,300 through the pullback
            from January's high, partly because prints like this one keep a rate cut on the table,
            even with the Fed on hold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="body reveal">
          A stronger gold price doesn't just lift the metal — it lifts every rung of the ladder
          underneath it, the value of every ounce still in the ground. That's the difference
          between a junior that can fund its next drill program and one that's raising at a
          discount, and none of it shows up in a single company's news release.
        </p>

        <div class="stats reveal">
          <div>
            <p class="n">3.50–3.75%</p>
            <p class="k">Fed funds rate, unchanged since December 2025</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="n">0</p>
            <p class="k">Rate cuts delivered in 2026 so far</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="n">Sept. 16</p>
            <p class="k">Next scheduled Fed rate decision</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="body reveal">
          This is the half of the notes that has nothing to do with a drill rig. Before we read
          what hit SEDAR+ overnight, we read what hit Washington — a rate call re-rates the whole
          sector at once; one drill result only re-rates one company. Knowing which regime you're
          in decides where on the ladder you want to be: producers and near-term builders hold up
          best when the Fed stays hawkish and the metal has to do all the work on its own, while
          the grassroots and discovery-stage names are the ones that move first and hardest once a
          cut finally comes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ STAGE TABLE ============ -->
    <section class="sec">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">What stage a company is at — and why it changes everything</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Two companies can both call themselves gold explorers and have nothing in common. Know the
          stage and the headlines start making sense. It's what decides whether a good hole doubles
          the share price or barely moves it.
        </p>

        <div class="tbl-scroll reveal">
          <table>
            <thead>
