              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ HONEST ============ -->
    <section class="honest">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">The honest part</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Most exploration companies never find anything worth mining. That's not sector-bashing.
          It's the business model: a lot of holes get drilled so that a couple of them can pay for
          the rest. That 431% average is the survivors — the names that went nowhere don't make the
          ranking, and there are far more of them.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Then there's dilution. An explorer has no revenue, so it lives by issuing shares. You can
          be entirely right about the geology and still lose money, because by the time the mine gets
          built there are four times as many shares as when you bought. Then they roll it back ten to
          one and start again. Reading the financings matters more than reading the news release
          about them.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          And they get halted. Your stock stops trading pending news and reopens somewhere else
          entirely — could be up, could be down, and you don't get a vote. Anyone who tells you this
          part is manageable is selling something.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          None of that is a reason to stay out. It's the reason you check what stage a company is at
          and what it just filed, before you look at the chart. That's the job. It's what goes out in
          the group every morning.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ READING A HOLE ============ -->
    <section class="sec" style="padding-top:44px">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">How to read the one sentence that moves the stock</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Drill results come out as a single line of numbers, and almost nobody outside the industry
          knows how to read it. Here's a real one, from Prospector Metals' ML project in the Yukon —
          the name that topped this year's Venture 50 at +1,130%.
        </p>

        <div class="hole reveal">
          <p class="id">Hole ML31 · TESS zone · ML project, Yukon</p>
          <p class="cut"><b>44 m</b> of <b>13.79 g/t</b> gold, <b>1.89%</b> copper and <b>38.08 g/t</b> silver</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>44 m</dt>
            <dd>The length of mineralized rock the drill passed through — not how deep the hole went. Width is what turns a curiosity into something you could mine.</dd>
            <dt>13.79 g/t gold</dt>
            <dd>Grams of gold in every tonne of rock. Plenty of producing open pits run on about 1 g/t. This is roughly fourteen times that, held over forty-four metres.</dd>
            <dt>1.89% copper, 38.08 g/t silver</dt>
            <dd>The by-products. They pay part of the cost of getting the gold out, which is what keeps a deposit alive when the gold price turns.</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="fund">
            The company has three rigs turning and a fully funded 25,000 m drill program this season,
            budgeted at about C$15 million. Nobody spends that on a maybe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="body reveal">
          Width, grade, by-products, and whether there's money in the bank to keep drilling — that's
          the whole read. Those numbers come off two pieces of paper. Knowing when they're due is
          most of the edge you get from a laptop at the kitchen table.
        </p>

        <div class="plates">
          <figure class="reveal">
            <img
              src={core.src}
              width={PLATE_W}
              height={coreH}
              alt="An overhead photograph of a wooden drill core tray with split rock core, a handwritten core log sheet, a pencil and a hand lens."
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption>
              The core log. Core comes up, gets laid out in the tray, and somebody logs it metre by
              metre. Then it's sawn in half — one half goes to the lab, one half stays in the box so
              somebody can check the work later. That second half exists because of Bre-X, too.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure class="reveal">
            <img
              src={assay.src}
              width={PLATE_W}
              height={assayH}
              alt="An overhead photograph of numbered cloth sample bags and a printed laboratory assay certificate with a ruled results table."
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption>
              The assay certificate. Samples go to an independent lab and come back as numbers, and
              the day those numbers are released is usually the day the share price does something.
              Labs run queues, so the delay itself is information.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ LADDER + MORNING ============ -->
    <section class="sec">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="reveal">Where we are on the ladder right now</h2>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Gold printed US$5,542 an ounce in January, came off hard, and sits near US$4,350 today.
          That's still about 65% above where it opened 2025. Copper made a fresh all-time high on
          COMEX on Aug. 7. Uranium is in the mid-eighties, a level that looked absurd when the last
          cycle ended. Money follows that down the food chain the way it always does: first into the
          producers, then the developers, then the explorers who own ground near something that
          works. That's nearology, and it works every cycle.
        </p>
        <p class="body reveal">
          Risk climbs in that same order. So do the multiples. The bottom three rungs are where
          almost everything on the Venture actually sits — and where every name on that Venture 50
